    "title_en": "Quantum Complexity of Weighted Diameter and Radius in CONGEST Networks",
    "abstract_en": "This paper investigates the round complexity of computing the weighted diameter and radius of a graph in the quantum CONGEST model. We present a quantum algorithm that achieves a (<inline-formula xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"> <tex-math notation=\"LaTeX\">$1\\boldsymbol {+} {o}$ </tex-math></inline-formula> (1))-approximation of the diameter and radius with a round complexity of <inline-formula xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"> <tex-math notation=\"LaTeX\">$O$ </tex-math></inline-formula>(<inline-formula xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"> <tex-math notation=\"LaTeX\">${n} ^{\\text {3/4}}{D}^{\\text {1/4}}$ </tex-math></inline-formula>) up to logarithmic factors, where <inline-formula xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"> <tex-math notation=\"LaTeX\">$n$ </tex-math></inline-formula> is the number of nodes and <inline-formula xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"> <tex-math notation=\"LaTeX\">$D$ </tex-math></inline-formula> represents the unweighted diameter. This highlights the advantages of quantum communication over classical communication, as computing a (3/<inline-formula xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"> <tex-math notation=\"LaTeX\">$2\\boldsymbol {-} \\boldsymbol {\\varepsilon } $ </tex-math></inline-formula>)-approximation of the diameter and radius in a classical CONGEST network requires near-linear in <inline-formula xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"> <tex-math notation=\"LaTeX\">$n$ </tex-math></inline-formula> rounds according to Abboud et al. [DISC ’16], even when <inline-formula xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"> <tex-math notation=\"LaTeX\">$D$ </tex-math></inline-formula> is constant. On the other hand, we establish a lower bound of <inline-formula xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"> <tex-math notation=\"LaTeX\">$\\boldsymbol {\\Omega } $ </tex-math></inline-formula>(<inline-formula xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"> <tex-math notation=\"LaTeX\">${n} ^{\\text {2/3}}$ </tex-math></inline-formula>) up to logarithmic factors, for (3/<inline-formula xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"> <tex-math notation=\"LaTeX\">$2\\boldsymbol {-} \\boldsymbol {\\varepsilon } $ </tex-math></inline-formula>)-approximating the weighted diameter/radius in quantum CONGEST networks, even when <inline-formula xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"> <tex-math notation=\"LaTeX\">${D} \\boldsymbol {=} \\boldsymbol {\\Theta } $ </tex-math></inline-formula>(<inline-formula xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"> <tex-math notation=\"LaTeX\">$\\boldsymbol {\\log {n}}$ </tex-math></inline-formula>). Consequently, in quantum CONGEST networks, computing the weighted diameter and radius of graphs with small <inline-formula xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"> <tex-math notation=\"LaTeX\">$D$ </tex-math></inline-formula> proves to be strictly harder than their unweighted counterparts, as compared to Le Gall and Magniez’s algorithm for unweighted diameter/radius, which runs in near-square-root-<italic xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">nD</i> rounds [PODC ’16].",
